// case: call-wrong-arity
// what: `add` takes two parameters. Calling it with three arguments must not
//      compile; the extra argument used to be dropped and the call emitted.
//
//      Both ends of the comparison were available the whole time and nothing
//      joined them: the call had a name and a list of arguments, the
//      declaration was a different node in a different pass, and the
//      argument types the checker inferred on the way past were thrown away.
//      `Symbol` now carries the declared parameter list, which is what makes
//      the check possible at all.
// expect-error: Wrong number of arguments to add: it takes 2, got 3
fn add(a: int, b: int) -> int { return a + b }

fn main() -> int { return add(1, 2, 3) }
